{
  "case_id": "2009-08-6925",
  "document_hash": "ba0c55e51848a460ae6dccd2050059cae473b36e4420daf210296db16bd0383d",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Lisa Call, Esq., on behalf of the Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2009-08-6925",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba0c55e51848a460ae6dccd2050059cae473b36e4420daf210296db16bd0383d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba0c55e51848-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. CALL: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: Mr. Johnson's conviction for battery in the State of Florida can be sustained by the slightest contact. Such a conviction does not qualify as a violent felony under the Armed Career Criminal Act. A violent felony means one that has as an element the use, attempted use, or threatened use of physical force against the person of another. Physical contact is not the same as physical force."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2009-08-6925",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba0c55e51848a460ae6dccd2050059cae473b36e4420daf210296db16bd0383d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba0c55e51848-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We will hear argument next in Case 08-6925, Johnson v. United States. Ms. Call."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-6925",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba0c55e51848a460ae6dccd2050059cae473b36e4420daf210296db16bd0383d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba0c55e51848-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: And that's -- you say that's Federal law. Would it make a difference if, contra what Florida held in the Hearns case, a State had typed a battery statute as a crime of violence?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2009-08-6925",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba0c55e51848a460ae6dccd2050059cae473b36e4420daf210296db16bd0383d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba0c55e51848-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: But what -- what -- before we can even get into this, I think we have to decide if those words \"striking or touching\" describe one crime or two."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2009-08-6925",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba0c55e51848a460ae6dccd2050059cae473b36e4420daf210296db16bd0383d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba0c55e51848-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 14,
        "start": 14
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Can I ask you something? In your definition, you appear to hinge it on the fact that the force used has to cause injury of some type, that. That appears to be the only definition you can give."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-6925",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba0c55e51848a460ae6dccd2050059cae473b36e4420daf210296db16bd0383d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba0c55e51848-0000-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Are there any States in which the only prosecution for battery that can be brought is under a statute as broad as this one, which is -- is covered by even a touching?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2009-08-6925",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba0c55e51848a460ae6dccd2050059cae473b36e4420daf210296db16bd0383d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba0c55e51848-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. CALL: Your Honor, I would have two responses to that. The first is, in the second prong, Congress used the word \"conduct.\" And if they had meant conduct to be included in 1 rather than the elemental definition, that would have been a very easy definition."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-6925",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba0c55e51848a460ae6dccd2050059cae473b36e4420daf210296db16bd0383d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba0c55e51848-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: You -- you -- you would have us believe that by \"violent felony\" in this -- in this statute, Congress meant the threat -- the threat? It doesn't even have to be the act."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2009-08-6925",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba0c55e51848a460ae6dccd2050059cae473b36e4420daf210296db16bd0383d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba0c55e51848-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: May I ask -- may I ask this, following up on Justice Scalia's point? Are there any other crimes other than battery that -- that create the same situation where in the first time it is committed it's a misdemeanor, but then it becomes a felony because it's committed twice? So what we have here is conduct that first time is misdemeanor, second time is a felony. Are there any other examples of crimes that fit that category that come within Subparagraph 1?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2009-08-6925",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba0c55e51848a460ae6dccd2050059cae473b36e4420daf210296db16bd0383d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba0c55e51848-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "One of the principal vices of Petitioner's arguments is also that it would leave the Federal domestic violence provisions like Section 922(g)(9) with relatively patchwork and haphazard application, which is precisely one of the considerations that motivated this Court's decision just last term in the United States v. Hayes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-6925",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba0c55e51848a460ae6dccd2050059cae473b36e4420daf210296db16bd0383d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba0c55e51848-0002-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The district court rested its decision on both Subsections 1 and 2, and the court of appeals addressed only Subsection 1."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2009-08-6925",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba0c55e51848a460ae6dccd2050059cae473b36e4420daf210296db16bd0383d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba0c55e51848-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: How -- I'm sorry. Before you go on, your adversary claims you waived by not raising this as an -- as an alternative in the sorts stage."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2009-08-6925",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ba0c55e51848a460ae6dccd2050059cae473b36e4420daf210296db16bd0383d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ba0c55e51848-0002-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "it raised his guideline range, from 27 to 33 months, to a mandatory minimum sentence of 15 years."
    },
